<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  /Library/LaunchDaemons/tierstore-fs.plist: `tierstore fs` as a LaunchDaemon on a Mac, the virtual
  build file system's NFSv3 frontend (docs/design/virtual-build-fs.md section 8). It serves the NFS
  and MOUNT protocols on loopback only (`fs.nfs.listen`), mounts the export read-only at
  `fs.mountpoint` as `fs.nfs.mount_user`, and unmounts on SIGTERM. root:wheel, mode 0644.

  The job runs as _tierstore, the user and group of tierstore.plist, because the fill socket of the
  store answers only a peer of its own user: a job of any other user, root included, has every fill
  refused. Nothing of it needs root: the NFS socket is on an unprivileged loopback port, and
  `mount_nfs`, `umount` and `diskutil unmount force` of a loopback export need none when the mount
  user owns the mount point. `fs.nfs.mount_user` is left out of the file (the user of the process
  is the default), and the mount point, `fs.mountpoint`, is a directory _tierstore owns, which an
  operator makes with the file. The file is /usr/local/tierstore/etc/fs.toml, which has the `[fs]`
  table and holds no secret and no key file (its directory is root:_tierstore, mode 0750); no
  environment variable is set, the process reads none, and launchd needs no notification of
  readiness.

  The program is the copy of the current release's binary that the update hook puts at
  /usr/local/libexec/tierstore/tierstore for tierstore.plist, the same binary as that job's.

  This job is not in hostkeeper's package yet, as tierstore-fs@.service is not for Linux: the
  update hook learns it with the two alternating jobs that the hand-over of the NFS socket
  (`fs.nfs.handoff_socket`) needs. Until then an operator installs the file, and it restarts in
  place with a gap in serving: a hard mount makes the clients wait, and the restarted process
  finds the mount, reopens its table of tree numbers and serves on the same port.

  launchd sends SIGTERM on bootout. The process unmounts, by force if a job still uses the mount,
  and exits 0; ExitTimeOut (30 s) is far more than that takes. KeepAlive starts the process again whenever it exits while the job is
  loaded, at most once in ThrottleInterval (10 s).

  crates/tierstore/tests/packaging_hostkeeper.rs parses this file and checks it against the binary.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>tierstore-fs</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/local/libexec/tierstore/tierstore</string>
    <string>fs</string>
    <string>--config</string>
    <string>/usr/local/tierstore/etc/fs.toml</string>
  </array>
  <key>UserName</key>
  <string>_tierstore</string>
  <key>GroupName</key>
  <string>_tierstore</string>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>10</integer>
  <key>ExitTimeOut</key>
  <integer>30</integer>
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Standard</string>
  <key>Umask</key>
  <integer>63</integer>
  <!-- Jobs' overlays keep files of the mount open as long as a job runs; the limit bounds the distinct cache files held open, which the process shares across handles. -->
  <key>SoftResourceLimits</key>
  <dict>
    <key>NumberOfFiles</key>
    <integer>1048576</integer>
  </dict>
  <key>HardResourceLimits</key>
  <dict>
    <key>NumberOfFiles</key>
    <integer>1048576</integer>
  </dict>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/usr/local/tierstore/log/tierstore-fs.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/usr/local/tierstore/log/tierstore-fs.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
